  "path_family_probe": {
    "actual_cause": "The sink is lexically inside an anonymous callback, so its enclosing method is the closure rather than the labeled function, and the verdict's `sinkMethod == function` filter drops it. On angular-http-server the sink `fs.readFileSync(possibleFilename)` sits in the callback passed to fs.stat, and Joern names its method `<lambda>0`; verdict(function='requestListener') returns None while verdict(function='') returns model_entailed. JavaScript is callback-heavy, so the labeled function and the method containing the sink routinely differ.",
    "correct_fix": [
      "scope a sink to any method lexically nested within the labeled function, not only the labeled function itself",
      "scope parameter sources to the labeled function's own parameters, not every enclosing or lambda parameter",
      "then re-measure: only a witness naming the real source should count"
    ],
    "method": "the same 8 path pairs, run with and without req.url/request.url/req.path/url.parse added to the source facts",
    "question": "Does the missing `req.url` source token explain path's 0/8?",
    "result": {
      "with_req_url_added": "0/8",
      "with_shipped_facts": "0/8"
    },
    "verdict": "FALSIFIED. The source token was not the cause, and adding it converts nothing.",
    "why_the_obvious_fix_is_wrong": "Relaxing the filter alone raises the number with a FALSE witness. With parameter sources on, the flows found in that closure are `res -> fs.readFileSync(...)` and `this -> ...`, neither of which is the vulnerability -- the real path is req.url -> url -> resolveUrl(...) -> possibleFilename -> sink, which also crosses a helper call. Turning a miss into a wrong entailment is worse than the miss: it is the arbiter asserting something untrue, which is the one failure mode this architecture exists to prevent."
  },
